  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⚔️ Sword Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a hero's blade, a cursed one, or something dug out of a barrow: Oathwarden, Griefbane, Sigeling."
    },
    "intro": "A sword with a name is a sword with a story. This **sword name generator** gives you names in three moods: heroic blades like <em>Oathwarden</em>, dark and cursed ones like <em>Griefbane</em>, and old swords in the style of Beowulf's <em>Hrunting</em>. Add an <a href=\"epithet-generator.html\">epithet</a> for its reputation, or a description of what it looks like.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Famous swords and their names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Excalibur</strong>, King Arthur's sword, is <em>Caliburnus</em> in Geoffrey of Monmouth, probably from the Welsh <em>Caledfwlch</em>.</li><li><strong>Durendal</strong> was Roland's sword in the <em>Song of Roland</em>; he tried to break it rather than let it fall to the enemy, and couldn't. Charlemagne's was <strong>Joyeuse</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tizona</strong> and <strong>Colada</strong> belonged to El Cid. A sword said to be Tizona is in a museum in Burgos.</li><li><strong>Gram</strong>, Sigurd's sword, means \"wrath\". It was broken and reforged before he killed the <a href=\"dragon-name-generator.html\">dragon</a> Fáfnir.</li><li><strong>Tyrfing</strong>, in Norse legend, was cursed: it could never be drawn without killing someone.</li><li><strong>Kusanagi</strong>, the \"grass-cutter\", is one of the three sacred treasures of the Japanese imperial family.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Three kinds of sword",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Heroic</strong> names promise something: <em>Oathwarden</em>, <em>Hopelight</em>, <em>Lionguard</em>. The sword of someone who means well.</li><li><strong>Dark and cursed</strong> names threaten: <em>Griefbane</em>, <em>Widowmaker</em>, <em>Gravethirst</em>. Michael Moorcock's Stormbringer, which drinks the souls of those it kills, set the standard.</li><li><strong>Old English</strong> names end in <em>-ing</em>, like the swords in <em>Beowulf</em>: Hrunting, the borrowed sword that fails against Grendel's mother, and Nægling, which breaks in the fight with the dragon. These use Old English words like <em>hild</em> (battle), <em>gār</em> (spear), <em>īsen</em> (iron), <em>glēd</em> (ember) and <em>sigel</em> (the sun).</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How swords were made, and why it matters",
        "content": "<p>Early medieval swords were often <strong>pattern-welded</strong>: rods of iron and steel twisted and forged together, leaving swirling patterns on the blade like water or snakeskin. To the people who carried them, a pattern-welded sword looked alive, which may be one reason they were given names.</p><p>Some Viking-age blades carry their maker's name inlaid in the steel: the swords marked <em>+VLFBERH+T</em> are the most famous. A sword named after its smith is an old idea.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming your own sword",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>What did it do?</strong> Name it after its greatest deed, or its worst.</li><li><strong>Who made it?</strong> A smith's mark, a god's forge, a <a href=\"dwarf-name-generator.html\">dwarf</a>'s grudge.</li><li><strong>Who carried it before?</strong> Swords outlive their owners. A sword with three names, one from each bearer, is a quest in itself.</li><li><strong>Other styles</strong>: for blades with real Elvish meanings, try the <a href=\"elven-sword-name-generator.html\">Elven Sword Name Generator</a>; for Japanese names, the <a href=\"japanese-sword-name-generator.html\">Japanese Sword Name Generator</a>.</li><li><strong>Who carries it?</strong> An Old English sword needs an Old English owner: try the <a href=\"anglo-saxon-name-generator.html\">Anglo-Saxon Name Generator</a> or the <a href=\"viking-name-generator.html\">Viking Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
